# Managed by infra-hetzner/ansible/roles/app — do not edit by hand.
# Re-rendered on every `ansible-playbook --tags app` run from
# `allowed_client_cns` in group_vars/all.yml.
#
# Imported into Caddyfile via `import snippets/cn_allowlist.snippet`.
# Defines the `@allowed_clients` request matcher; the site block
# uses it to gate the reverse_proxy → CH.
#
# ## Why we match on full DN, not just CN
#
# Caddy 2.x does NOT register a `{http.request.tls.client.subject_cn}`
# placeholder. The available placeholders are documented in Caddy
# source (modules/caddyhttp/replacer.go): fingerprint, public_key,
# public_key_sha256, issuer, serial, subject, certificate_pem,
# certificate_der_base64, san.*. `subject` returns the full
# RFC-2253 Distinguished Name string (pkix.Name.String()), e.g.
# `CN=alice-laptop` for a cert issued by the bundled
# issue-client-cert.sh script (which sets Subject to `/CN=<name>`
# with no other RDNs).
#
# A matcher referencing a non-existent placeholder evaluates to
# null in CEL, `.matches()` on null errors, and `Match()` then
# returns false — fail-closed, but breaks every legitimate client.
# This snippet therefore matches against `subject` directly with
# the `CN=` prefix.
#
# ## Defense-in-depth regex
#
# The first matcher asserts the subject is well-formed
# (`^CN=[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$`) — same character set the issuance
# script and Ansible preflight enforce. This blocks any CEL
# injection via a CN crafted by an attacker who has compromised
# the CA key.

@allowed_clients expression `{http.request.tls.client.subject}.matches("^CN=[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$") && {http.request.tls.client.subject} in [{% for cn in allowed_client_cns %}"CN={{ cn }}"{%- if not loop.last %},{% endif %}{% endfor %}]`
